1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,000
Welcome back. When you build on the Cloud API,

2
00:00:03,000 --> 00:00:05,000
cost hides in plain sight.

3
00:00:05,000 --> 00:00:08,000
Every response hands you the exact token counts,

4
00:00:08,000 --> 00:00:10,000
but it's easy to read them wrong and

5
00:00:10,000 --> 00:00:12,000
model your spend off the wrong number.

6
00:00:12,000 --> 00:00:15,000
This lesson covers what each usage field

7
00:00:15,000 --> 00:00:18,000
means and how to count tokens before you call.

8
00:00:18,000 --> 00:00:20,000
Let's dig in. Let's lock down

9
00:00:20,000 --> 00:00:22,000
three terms before anything else

10
00:00:22,000 --> 00:00:25,000
because everything downstream builds on them.

11
00:00:25,000 --> 00:00:28,000
First, the usage object itself.

12
00:00:28,000 --> 00:00:31,000
It rides back on every single response

13
00:00:31,000 --> 00:00:34,000
and it reports the token counts for that one call.

14
00:00:34,000 --> 00:00:37,000
That's your source of truth for spend.

15
00:00:37,000 --> 00:00:39,000
Second, input tokens.

16
00:00:39,000 --> 00:00:41,000
This one's subtle, so hold on to it.

17
00:00:41,000 --> 00:00:44,000
It counts the uncached input,

18
00:00:44,000 --> 00:00:48,000
the prompt tokens the model actually read fresh at full price.

19
00:00:48,000 --> 00:00:50,000
When caching kicks in,

20
00:00:50,000 --> 00:00:51,000
it isn't your whole prompt.

21
00:00:51,000 --> 00:00:54,000
Third, output tokens.

22
00:00:54,000 --> 00:00:58,000
These are the tokens the model generated in its reply,

23
00:00:58,000 --> 00:00:59,000
and on every current model,

24
00:00:59,000 --> 00:01:02,000
they carry a higher price than input.

25
00:01:02,000 --> 00:01:06,000
Usage object, input tokens, output tokens.

26
00:01:06,000 --> 00:01:09,000
Get those three straight and the rest follows.

27
00:01:09,000 --> 00:01:12,000
Where does this data actually come from?

28
00:01:12,000 --> 00:01:15,000
The nice part is you don't make a second call to get it.

29
00:01:15,000 --> 00:01:17,000
The usage object comes back attached to

30
00:01:17,000 --> 00:01:21,000
the same response as your content right there in the payload.

31
00:01:21,000 --> 00:01:23,000
But here's the catch worth internalizing.

32
00:01:23,000 --> 00:01:26,000
It reports counts for that one request only.

33
00:01:26,000 --> 00:01:28,000
It isn't a running total,

34
00:01:28,000 --> 00:01:31,000
and the API doesn't add anything up for you across calls.

35
00:01:31,000 --> 00:01:35,000
So if you want a daily spend number or a per user total,

36
00:01:35,000 --> 00:01:37,000
that aggregation is your job.

37
00:01:37,000 --> 00:01:40,000
Your code reads the usage off each response

38
00:01:40,000 --> 00:01:43,000
and writes those numbers into your own ledger.

39
00:01:43,000 --> 00:01:46,000
Whatever that is, a database row or a log line.

40
00:01:46,000 --> 00:01:50,000
Track it per call and your spend picture stays honest.

41
00:01:50,000 --> 00:01:54,000
Let's put input and output side-by-side

42
00:01:54,000 --> 00:01:55,000
because they're priced differently,

43
00:01:55,000 --> 00:01:58,000
and that difference drives your bill.

44
00:01:58,000 --> 00:02:01,000
Input tokens are everything the model reads.

45
00:02:01,000 --> 00:02:03,000
Hold that as the pre-caching picture

46
00:02:03,000 --> 00:02:05,000
because caching splits this reading side

47
00:02:05,000 --> 00:02:07,000
into separate fields.

48
00:02:07,000 --> 00:02:09,000
And we'll get to that split in a moment.

49
00:02:09,000 --> 00:02:13,000
It covers your system prompt, the full conversation history,

50
00:02:13,000 --> 00:02:16,000
your tool definitions, and the current message.

51
00:02:16,000 --> 00:02:19,000
All of it counts and it's billed at the base input rate.

52
00:02:19,000 --> 00:02:23,000
Output tokens are everything the model generates back,

53
00:02:23,000 --> 00:02:25,000
the visible answer, plus any thinking tokens

54
00:02:25,000 --> 00:02:27,000
it produced along the way.

55
00:02:27,000 --> 00:02:29,000
Here's the part that surprises people.

56
00:02:29,000 --> 00:02:33,000
On every current model, output is priced higher than input,

57
00:02:33,000 --> 00:02:34,000
several times higher in fact.

58
00:02:34,000 --> 00:02:38,000
So a short prompt that triggers a long, chatty response

59
00:02:38,000 --> 00:02:42,000
can cost far more than its small input suggests.

60
00:02:42,000 --> 00:02:43,000
Watch the output side.

61
00:02:44,000 --> 00:02:47,000
Now let's name all four fields you'll see

62
00:02:47,000 --> 00:02:50,000
because caching splits your input into pieces.

63
00:02:50,000 --> 00:02:54,000
First, input tokens, that's the uncached prompt,

64
00:02:54,000 --> 00:02:56,000
read at full input price.

65
00:02:56,000 --> 00:02:59,000
Second, output tokens, what the model generated

66
00:02:59,000 --> 00:03:01,000
at the higher output rate.

67
00:03:01,000 --> 00:03:04,000
Third, cache creation input tokens.

68
00:03:04,000 --> 00:03:07,000
These are prompt tokens written into the cache on this call

69
00:03:07,000 --> 00:03:11,000
and they carry a small write premium over base input.

70
00:03:11,000 --> 00:03:14,000
Fourth, cache read input tokens.

71
00:03:14,000 --> 00:03:16,000
These were served straight from the cache

72
00:03:16,000 --> 00:03:21,000
instead of reprocessed and they bill at a deep discount.

73
00:03:21,000 --> 00:03:24,000
So one logical prompt can land across three of these buckets

74
00:03:24,000 --> 00:03:26,000
at three different prices.

75
00:03:26,000 --> 00:03:28,000
Read all four every time.

76
00:03:29,000 --> 00:03:30,000
I want to slow down on input tokens

77
00:03:30,000 --> 00:03:33,000
because this is the single most common

78
00:03:33,000 --> 00:03:35,000
cost tracking mistake.

79
00:03:35,000 --> 00:03:36,000
The moment caching is on,

80
00:03:36,000 --> 00:03:39,000
input tokens stops meaning your whole prompt.

81
00:03:39,000 --> 00:03:41,000
It counts only the fresh part,

82
00:03:41,000 --> 00:03:43,000
the tokens that weren't already cached.

83
00:03:43,000 --> 00:03:45,000
The cached tokens have moved

84
00:03:45,000 --> 00:03:47,000
into those two cache fields instead.

85
00:03:47,000 --> 00:03:49,000
So if you glance at input tokens alone

86
00:03:49,000 --> 00:03:51,000
and call it your prompt size,

87
00:03:51,000 --> 00:03:53,000
you'll under count, sometimes badly.

88
00:03:53,000 --> 00:03:56,000
Picture a huge cached system prompt

89
00:03:56,000 --> 00:03:58,000
with a tiny new question on top.

90
00:03:58,000 --> 00:04:01,000
Input tokens look small, almost like nothing,

91
00:04:01,000 --> 00:04:04,000
even though the model attended to a massive context.

92
00:04:04,000 --> 00:04:06,000
So never read that one field in isolation

93
00:04:06,000 --> 00:04:08,000
and treat it as your full input.

94
00:04:08,000 --> 00:04:10,000
It's a remainder, not the total.

95
00:04:11,000 --> 00:04:14,000
So how do you get the real prompt size?

96
00:04:14,000 --> 00:04:17,000
You add three fields together, not just read one.

97
00:04:17,000 --> 00:04:19,000
Start with input tokens,

98
00:04:19,000 --> 00:04:21,000
the uncached remainder we just talked about.

99
00:04:21,000 --> 00:04:24,000
Then add cache creation input tokens,

100
00:04:24,000 --> 00:04:27,000
the ones written into the cache on this call.

101
00:04:27,000 --> 00:04:29,000
Then add cache read input tokens,

102
00:04:29,000 --> 00:04:32,000
the ones served from the cache.

103
00:04:32,000 --> 00:04:34,000
That sum, all three together,

104
00:04:34,000 --> 00:04:38,000
is the true input the model saw for this request.

105
00:04:38,000 --> 00:04:40,000
This matters for two reasons.

106
00:04:40,000 --> 00:04:43,000
One, it's the honest number for your cost math.

107
00:04:43,000 --> 00:04:46,000
Once you weight each piece by its price.

108
00:04:46,000 --> 00:04:50,000
Two, it tells you how full your context window really is,

109
00:04:50,000 --> 00:04:53,000
which pure input tokens would hide.

110
00:04:53,000 --> 00:04:55,000
Three fields, add it up.

111
00:04:55,000 --> 00:04:56,000
That's your actual prompt.

112
00:04:57,000 --> 00:05:01,000
Everything so far reads usage after the fact,

113
00:05:01,000 --> 00:05:04,000
but often you want the number before you spend anything.

114
00:05:04,000 --> 00:05:07,000
That's what the count tokens endpoint is for.

115
00:05:07,000 --> 00:05:10,000
You hand it a request and it returns the input token count

116
00:05:10,000 --> 00:05:12,000
without running any generation.

117
00:05:12,000 --> 00:05:14,000
So there's no output charge at all.

118
00:05:14,000 --> 00:05:16,000
It's a pure estimate.

119
00:05:16,000 --> 00:05:18,000
A few things to get right.

120
00:05:18,000 --> 00:05:21,000
Pass the same inputs you actually plan to send,

121
00:05:21,000 --> 00:05:25,000
the model, the messages, the system prompt, and your tools.

122
00:05:25,000 --> 00:05:27,000
So the count reflects the real call

123
00:05:27,000 --> 00:05:29,000
and not a stripped down version.

124
00:05:29,000 --> 00:05:32,000
And remember, counts are model specific.

125
00:05:32,000 --> 00:05:35,000
Pass the same model you'll infer with

126
00:05:35,000 --> 00:05:38,000
because a different model can tokenize the very same text

127
00:05:38,000 --> 00:05:40,000
into a different number of tokens.

128
00:05:40,000 --> 00:05:42,000
Estimate first, then send.

129
00:05:43,000 --> 00:05:45,000
One more layer, because the usage object

130
00:05:45,000 --> 00:05:48,000
can carry more than plain input and output.

131
00:05:48,000 --> 00:05:50,000
When your call uses server tools,

132
00:05:50,000 --> 00:05:53,000
that activity shows up in its own section

133
00:05:53,000 --> 00:05:57,000
under server tool use, separate from your text tokens.

134
00:05:57,000 --> 00:05:59,000
And when you attach tool definitions,

135
00:05:59,000 --> 00:06:02,000
those definitions quietly add system prompt tokens

136
00:06:02,000 --> 00:06:04,000
to your input before you've sent

137
00:06:04,000 --> 00:06:07,000
a single message of your own.

138
00:06:07,000 --> 00:06:09,000
The amount of that overhead varies by model

139
00:06:09,000 --> 00:06:11,000
and by your tool choice setting.

140
00:06:11,000 --> 00:06:14,000
So it isn't one fixed number you can memorize.

141
00:06:14,000 --> 00:06:17,000
The takeaway for cost modeling is simple.

142
00:06:17,000 --> 00:06:20,000
Don't count only the obvious input and output.

143
00:06:20,000 --> 00:06:23,000
Fold in the cache fields, the tool overhead,

144
00:06:23,000 --> 00:06:25,000
and any server tool activity.

145
00:06:25,000 --> 00:06:28,000
Miss a category and your estimate runs light,

146
00:06:28,000 --> 00:06:29,000
always in the wrong direction.

147
00:06:31,000 --> 00:06:35,000
The usage object turns every response into a cost record.

148
00:06:35,000 --> 00:06:36,000
It matters because those counts

149
00:06:36,000 --> 00:06:40,000
are the only honest basis for your spend math.

150
00:06:40,000 --> 00:06:44,000
One thing to watch, input tokens is the uncached remainder.

151
00:06:44,000 --> 00:06:48,000
So add all three input fields for true prompt sets.

152
00:06:48,000 --> 00:06:51,000
That covers reading the usage fields on a response.

153
00:06:51,000 --> 00:06:53,000
I'll see you in the next one.

